# Original prompt-specific planning and revision guidance, never model answers.
rows = r'''
narrative|E|Write a story about a character who finds a note in a borrowed library book. Make the note change one decision the character makes.|Establish what the character wants before the note appears, so its effect is visible.~Connect the discovery to a choice and a consequence; a sequence of unrelated adventures will not develop the task.~End with a detail that shows how the character's understanding has changed.
narrative|M|Write a story in which a team loses an important object just before a performance. Show how two different responses to the problem affect the outcome.|Choose the object and its practical importance before planning the crisis.~Give two characters contrasting actions, such as searching methodically and improvising, and let those actions cause later events.~Resolve the immediate problem while showing what the team learns about working together.
narrative|C|Write a story beginning with an apology that the narrator does not yet understand. Reveal its meaning through events rather than an explanation in the opening.|Plan the event behind the apology and decide which clues the narrator can notice early.~Reveal the cause gradually through actions or dialogue, keeping the narrator's knowledge consistent.~Return to the apology at the end so its meaning changes for the reader without an unrelated twist.
narrative|M|A character must deliver an ordinary parcel across an unfamiliar town. Write a story in which asking for help is more difficult than the journey itself.|Give the character a concrete reason to hesitate, such as embarrassment about being lost.~Use a specific obstacle that forces a choice about seeking help, then show its consequence.~Keep the parcel ordinary; develop tension through the character's decisions rather than unexplained magic.
narrative|C|Write a story told by a character who initially misjudges someone else's action. Let the reader notice clues to the mistake before the narrator does.|Plan the real motive and the narrator's mistaken interpretation separately.~Include observable clues that support the real motive without having the narrator explain them too soon.~Resolve the misunderstanding through evidence and show a changed response, avoiding a sudden unsupported revelation.
narrative|M|Write a story set during the final hour before a neighbourhood event. A small act of kindness must create an unexpected practical difficulty.|Choose a helpful action with a believable unintended consequence, rather than a random accident.~Build a causal chain from kindness to difficulty to a further decision.~Let the ending acknowledge both the good intention and the need to deal with its effects.
narrative|C|Write a story in which the same sound is comforting at the beginning and unsettling near the end. Make the change arise from what the character discovers.|Select a sound that can plausibly fit both moods, and establish its first meaning in context.~Use discovery to alter the character's interpretation while keeping the sound recognisably the same.~Finish with a consequence of that changed understanding rather than merely declaring the mood different.
persuasive|E|Write a letter asking your school to provide a sheltered outdoor reading area. Give two reasons and one practical suggestion.|Address the principal and state the request clearly before developing reasons.~Explain how shelter would affect reading or access, using plausible examples rather than invented survey results.~Suggest a workable location or trial and finish with a specific request for consideration.
persuasive|M|Your class can spend its excursion budget on a local field study or one large city exhibition. Recommend one option in a speech and respond fairly to a benefit of the other.|Identify the audience's goals, such as depth of learning, accessibility or cost, before choosing.~Develop distinct reasons with examples and acknowledge an actual strength of the alternative.~Explain why your chosen priorities still favour your recommendation; do not dismiss the other option as worthless.
persuasive|C|A council proposes charging a small fee for reserving public tennis courts while leaving unreserved use free. Write an opinion article supporting or opposing the policy, including a fair response to the strongest objection.|Distinguish reserved access from unreserved access so the argument addresses the actual policy.~Weigh fairness, upkeep or availability with concrete hypothetical examples rather than fabricated statistics.~Present a substantial opposing case and answer it with a safeguard, trade-off or reasoned priority.
persuasive|M|Should students choose one independent project each term? Write to the curriculum coordinator, explaining benefits and a realistic way to keep projects manageable.|State whether you support the proposal and define what choice would cover.~Develop benefits through examples of learning rather than repeating that choice is enjoyable.~Address time or resources with a practical boundary, then close with an actionable recommendation.
persuasive|C|Your town has funds for either repairing a small theatre or improving a riverside path. Write a submission recommending a priority while recognising who may lose out.|Choose criteria for priority, such as access, frequency of use or urgency, without inventing local facts.~Compare how the alternatives meet those criteria and identify a real cost to the group not prioritised.~Offer a reasonable next step for that group and explain why your recommendation remains justified.
persuasive|M|Write a speech arguing whether a school canteen should test one student-designed menu item each month. Include a condition that would make the trial fair.|Choose a position and explain effects on participation and everyday canteen operation.~Use a hypothetical item to make the proposal concrete while considering cost or dietary access.~Define a fair selection or feedback process rather than assuming every proposed item can be sold.
persuasive|C|Write an editorial on whether a prize should reward the best final product or the greatest improvement. Explain a fair method of judging your preferred approach.|Define best product and improvement so the comparison is clear.~Develop the value and limitation of each approach, recognising that judging improvement needs a starting point.~Propose evidence and criteria that support your preference, then address a credible fairness objection.
descriptive|E|Describe a familiar playground before anyone arrives in the morning. Use details from at least three senses and keep a consistent viewpoint.|Choose a position from which the whole description can unfold, such as beside the gate.~Select sensory details that suit an empty morning setting rather than listing unrelated adjectives.~Arrange details spatially or from arrival to departure, and finish with one change that suggests the day beginning.
descriptive|M|Describe a busy kitchen from the viewpoint of someone waiting for an important announcement. Let the ordinary details reflect the waiting person's mood.|Decide what the person hopes or fears without turning the piece into a complete explanation of the announcement.~Choose sounds, movements and objects that the waiting person would notice more intensely.~Use sentence rhythm and selective detail to sustain anticipation while keeping the kitchen physically clear.
descriptive|C|Describe the same market stall twice: once through the eyes of an excited visitor and once through the eyes of the tired stallholder. Keep the physical objects consistent.|List a few fixed objects, then decide which each observer would value or resent.~Change focus, comparisons and rhythm to express viewpoint while preserving those shared objects.~Make the contrast perceptible through description rather than simply labelling one person excited and the other tired.
descriptive|M|Describe a railway platform during the minute after a train has left. Create a shift from movement to stillness without narrating a new adventure.|Begin with traces of departure such as fading sound or moving air.~Guide attention from distant movement towards small nearby details as activity subsides.~Maintain the short time frame and use the final image to establish the new quiet.
descriptive|C|Describe an ordinary room so that the reader gradually realises someone important has recently left it. Do not state the departure directly.|Choose traces of absence that can be observed, such as a missing object or an interrupted routine.~Order details so their combined meaning emerges gradually rather than through a single explicit announcement.~Keep the emotional effect grounded in precise details and avoid contradictions about what remains.
descriptive|M|Describe a garden just after heavy rain, concentrating on contrasts between delicate details and larger movement.|Select a clear route for the reader's attention, such as from a leaf to the path to the drain.~Contrast small retained drops with broader runoff using accurate verbs and varied sentence lengths.~Use the contrast to create a coherent atmosphere rather than a catalogue of unrelated images.
expository|E|Explain to a younger pupil how to prepare for a group presentation. Organise your advice in a useful sequence and explain why two steps matter.|Identify the audience's likely needs and begin with understanding the topic and shared purpose.~Sequence planning, role allocation, practice and checking materials, explaining the purpose of at least two steps.~Use clear linking words and supportive language without writing a fictional story in place of advice.
expository|M|Explain why two people can remember the same event differently without either deliberately lying. Use an invented everyday example and acknowledge a limit to memory.|Distinguish deliberate deception from differences in attention or viewpoint.~Develop a concrete example showing which details each observer notices and why.~Explain that confidence does not by itself settle accuracy, and suggest checking another source where useful.
expository|C|Explain the difference between a result being possible, likely and certain. Use one connected example and show how new information can change the judgement.|Define the three terms with care, avoiding the claim that likely means guaranteed.~Keep one scenario, such as choosing a coloured token, and vary the available evidence or conditions.~Show what additional information changes the conclusion, using internally consistent examples rather than unsupported percentages.
expository|M|Explain how a class could choose between two fundraising ideas fairly. Your explanation must include criteria, evidence and a decision process.|Name decision criteria such as cost, likely effort and who can participate.~Describe how to collect usable evidence without treating guesses as facts.~Explain how the group will compare the evidence and resolve disagreement using the chosen criteria.
expository|C|Explain why a fair comparison changes one important factor at a time. Use a classroom design example and discuss one uncertainty that may remain.|Choose two versions of a design and identify the single factor being compared.~Explain which conditions must stay similar so a difference is easier to interpret.~Name a remaining uncertainty, such as inconsistent measurement, and explain why one trial may not settle the question.
expository|C|Explain how a rule can be applied consistently yet still produce an unfair result. Give a balanced example and suggest a reasoned revision.|Separate consistency in applying a rule from fairness of its effects.~Construct a specific example in which different starting conditions matter, acknowledging what the original rule was trying to achieve.~Suggest a revision that addresses the problem while considering a new trade-off it might create.
informative|E|Write a welcome guide for a new pupil joining your classroom. Explain three routines and how to seek help when unsure.|Choose routines a newcomer needs immediately, such as arrival, materials and asking questions.~Use headings or clear paragraphs and give specific, invented classroom procedures consistently.~Include a supportive route to help rather than assuming every newcomer already understands school terms.
informative|M|Write an information page about a fictional community repair afternoon. Include what visitors may bring, what volunteers can and cannot promise, and how to book.|Define an internally consistent event with time, location and booking method.~Give useful examples of acceptable items and clear limits without making unsupported safety guarantees.~Organise practical details for quick reading and ensure no booking instruction contradicts the event information.
informative|C|Write a report on a fictional class trial comparing two reading corners. Invent a small, clearly labelled set of observations and distinguish findings from recommendations.|State the trial setting and label all invented observations as fictional.~Report consistent observations about use or comfort without claiming they prove every pupil's preference.~Separate what was observed from a cautious recommendation and name one limitation of the trial.
informative|M|Write a visitor guide to an imagined small museum. Describe three exhibits, a useful route and one accessibility arrangement.|Choose a theme that connects the exhibits and makes their descriptions coherent.~Give a route readers can follow without contradictory directions.~Describe a specific accessibility feature and how a visitor can request help, avoiding vague promises that every need is already met.
informative|C|Write a fact sheet about an invented animal adapted to a cold, windy island. Clearly label it fictional and explain how three features help it survive.|Define the island conditions and explicitly identify the animal as invented.~Connect each physical or behavioural feature to a particular environmental challenge rather than listing unrelated abilities.~Keep the biology internally plausible and distinguish habitat, diet and adaptations using clear sections.
informative|M|Write instructions for a fictional book-swap day. Include eligibility, a step-by-step exchange process and what happens to leftover books.|Set clear entry conditions such as book condition and suitable categories.~Describe the exchange sequence from arrival to selection so participants know what to do.~Explain leftover handling and make sure it is compatible with your earlier ownership and donation rules.
creative|M|Write a short piece narrated by a lost glove in a school office. Give the glove a distinctive voice and reveal something about its owner through remembered details.|Choose a voice consistent with the glove's imagined personality and limited viewpoint.~Reveal the owner through specific remembered actions rather than a list of traits.~Give the piece a small development or discovery so the object voice serves a purpose beyond the initial joke.
creative|C|Write a piece in which a town's shadows refuse to follow their owners for one afternoon. Establish a clear rule for the event and explore one consequence in depth.|Decide what the shadows can and cannot do, then apply that rule consistently.~Follow one character's response so the unusual idea produces a developed consequence.~Use an ending that grows from the established rule rather than cancelling the premise without explanation.
creative|M|Write a dialogue between a map and a compass that disagree about how to help a lost traveller. Let each reveal a useful limitation as well as a strength.|Give each object knowledge appropriate to its function, such as routes for the map and direction for the compass.~Develop disagreement through their different strengths rather than repeated insults.~Resolve or sharpen the disagreement by showing how the traveller needs more than one kind of information.
creative|C|Write a scene in a shop that sells forgotten minutes. Make the unusual idea reveal a character's priorities without explaining the entire world.|Choose a concrete desire that makes the character enter the shop.~Introduce enough rules for one meaningful choice, with a clear cost or limitation.~Let the choice show what the character values, and keep the focus on that scene rather than an encyclopedia of the setting.
creative|M|Write a letter from a tree to the people who sit beneath it. Include observations from two seasons and one request that follows from those observations.|Choose a tree's plausible fixed viewpoint and personify it consistently.~Use seasonal details to show changes in the people or place.~Make the request arise from what the tree has observed, keeping the letter coherent rather than listing unrelated wishes.
creative|C|Write two brief diary entries by the same character, before and after discovering that an admired invention has an unexpected cost. Let the language show the shift in judgement.|Plan the invention's attraction and a specific cost that complicates it.~Use the first entry to establish sincere enthusiasm and the second to reconsider with evidence.~Retain some continuity in the character's voice while showing a more qualified judgement instead of a simple unexplained reversal.
'''

for row in rows.strip().splitlines():
    mode, difficulty, prompt, working = row.split('|')
    add('writing', 'writing-'+mode, difficulty, prompt+' Allow 15 minutes in total, including any planning and editing you choose to do.', [], working.split('~'), topic=mode.title()+' composition')
